the target language is: lps.

% Checking the record of a post-trade workflow, in Logical English for LPS.
%
% Source: ex-cdm-swaps, Digital Asset's example of interest rate swaps and
% credit default swaps managed through their lives with ISDA's Common Domain
% Model on a Daml ledger (github.com/digital-asset-archive/ex-cdm-swaps,
% Apache License 2.0; see README.md and sources/). Its files hold 57 new
% trades, 9 later events (terminations, partial terminations, novations) and
% the master agreements between the parties.
%
% A post-trade event goes through three stages on that ledger. One party
% proposes it; every other party to it accepts; the ledger applies it, on
% its effective date. This program reads a record of those stages, one
% event at a time, and keeps the state of every trade: whether it is live,
% who holds it, and its notional. Each rule of the workflow is a law that
% records a breach where the record breaks the rule, at the event that
% breaks it, so the timeline shows where the record went wrong and the
% explanation of a breach names the rule and the facts it rests on.
%
% The scenario "the demo record" is the record of the files, dated by their
% own event dates, with the acceptances that the example's demonstration
% describes (docs/demo.md). It breaks no rule. Each other scenario changes
% that record in one way, and shows one rule at work.
%
% Dates are compared by the companion file trade_lifecycle_record.lps.

the maximum time is 160.

the events are:
    the day *a date* begins; known as day_begins.
    *a party* proposes the new trade *a trade* with *a second party* for *an amount*; known as propose_new_trade.
    *a party* proposes *an event* to terminate *a trade* effective on *a date*; known as propose_termination.
    *a party* proposes *an event* to terminate *an amount* of *a trade* effective on *a date*; known as propose_partial_termination.
    *a party* proposes *an event* to novate *an amount* of *a trade* from *a second party* to *a third party* as *a second trade* effective on *a date*; known as propose_novation.
    *a party* accepts *an event*; known as accepts.
    the ledger applies *an event*; known as applies.

the fluents are:
    the current date is *a date*; known as current_date.
    *a party* is a party to *an event*; known as party_to.
    *a party* has accepted *an event*; known as accepted.
    *an event* takes effect on *a date*; known as takes_effect.
    *an event* would book *a trade* between *a party* and *a second party* for *an amount*; known as would_book.
    *an event* would end *a trade*; known as would_end.
    *an event* would reduce *a trade* by *an amount*; known as would_reduce.
    *an event* would move *an amount* of *a trade* from *a party* to *a second party* as *a second trade*; known as would_move.
    *an event* has been applied; known as applied.
    *a trade* is live; known as live.
    *a party* holds *a trade*; known as holds_trade.
    the notional of *a trade* is *an amount*; known as notional.
    *an event* breaches the rule *a rule*; known as breach.

the templates are:
    *a party* and *a second party* have a master agreement.
    *a party* and *a second party* are covered by a master agreement.
    *a date* is later than *a second date*; known as date_later.

the knowledge base trade lifecycle record includes:

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% The stages of an event.

when the day a date begins
    and the current date is an old date
then the current date that is the old date becomes the date.

when the day a date begins
    and it is not the case that the current date is an old date
then the current date is the date.

when a party proposes the new trade a trade with a second party for an amount
then the party is a party to the trade
    and the second party is a party to the trade
    and the party has accepted the trade
    and the trade would book the trade between the party and the second party for the amount.

when a party proposes an event to terminate a trade effective on a date
    and a holder holds the trade
then the holder is a party to the event
    and the party has accepted the event
    and the event would end the trade
    and the event takes effect on the date.

when a party proposes an event to terminate an amount of a trade effective on a date
    and a holder holds the trade
then the holder is a party to the event
    and the party has accepted the event
    and the event would reduce the trade by the amount
    and the event takes effect on the date.

when a party proposes an event to novate an amount of a trade from a second party to a third party as a second trade effective on a date
    and a holder holds the trade
then the holder is a party to the event
    and the third party is a party to the event
    and the party has accepted the event
    and the event would move the amount of the trade from the second party to the third party as the second trade
    and the event takes effect on the date.

when a party accepts an event
then the party has accepted the event.

when the ledger applies an event
then the event has been applied.

% What applying an event does to the trades.

when the ledger applies an event
    and the event would book a trade between a party and a second party for an amount
then the trade is live
    and the party holds the trade
    and the second party holds the trade
    and the notional of the trade is the amount.

when the ledger applies an event
    and the event would end a trade
then it is not the case that the trade is live.

when the ledger applies an event
    and the event would reduce a trade by an amount
then the notional of the trade that is an old amount becomes old amount - amount.

when the ledger applies an event
    and the event would move an amount of a trade from a party to a second party as a second trade
    and a holder holds the trade
    and the holder is different from the party
then the second trade is live
    and the holder holds the second trade
    and the second party holds the second trade
    and the notional of the second trade is the amount.

when the ledger applies an event
    and the event would move an amount of a trade from a party to a second party as a second trade
    and the notional of the trade is a notional
    and the amount < the notional
then the notional of the trade that is an old amount becomes old amount - amount.

when the ledger applies an event
    and the event would move an amount of a trade from a party to a second party as a second trade
    and the notional of the trade is the amount
then it is not the case that the trade is live.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% The rules of the workflow. Each records a breach at the event that breaks
% it.

% A new trade is between parties with a master agreement.

when a party proposes the new trade a trade with a second party for an amount
    and it is not the case that the party and the second party are covered by a master agreement
then the trade breaches the rule a_master_agreement.

% A novation moves the trade to a party with a master agreement with the
% party that stays.

when a party proposes an event to novate an amount of a trade from a second party to a third party as a second trade effective on a date
    and a holder holds the trade
    and the holder is different from the second party
    and it is not the case that the holder and the third party are covered by a master agreement
then the event breaches the rule a_master_agreement.

% An event is proposed by a party to it.

when a party proposes an event to terminate a trade effective on a date
    and it is not the case that the party holds the trade
then the event breaches the rule proposed_by_a_party.

when a party proposes an event to terminate an amount of a trade effective on a date
    and it is not the case that the party holds the trade
then the event breaches the rule proposed_by_a_party.

when a party proposes an event to novate an amount of a trade from a second party to a third party as a second trade effective on a date
    and it is not the case that the party holds the trade
then the event breaches the rule proposed_by_a_party.

% An event changes a trade that is live.

when a party proposes an event to terminate a trade effective on a date
    and it is not the case that the trade is live
then the event breaches the rule a_live_trade.

when a party proposes an event to terminate an amount of a trade effective on a date
    and it is not the case that the trade is live
then the event breaches the rule a_live_trade.

when a party proposes an event to novate an amount of a trade from a second party to a third party as a second trade effective on a date
    and it is not the case that the trade is live
then the event breaches the rule a_live_trade.

% A partial termination takes off less than the notional; a novation moves
% no more than the notional, from a party that holds the trade.

when a party proposes an event to terminate an amount of a trade effective on a date
    and the notional of the trade is a notional
    and the amount >= the notional
then the event breaches the rule less_than_the_notional.

when a party proposes an event to novate an amount of a trade from a second party to a third party as a second trade effective on a date
    and the notional of the trade is a notional
    and the amount > the notional
then the event breaches the rule no_more_than_the_notional.

when a party proposes an event to novate an amount of a trade from a second party to a third party as a second trade effective on a date
    and it is not the case that the second party holds the trade
then the event breaches the rule from_a_holder.

% Only a party to an event accepts it.

when a party accepts an event
    and it is not the case that the party is a party to the event
then the event breaches the rule accepted_by_a_party.

% The ledger applies an event once, after every party has accepted it, and
% not before its effective date.

when the ledger applies an event
    and a party is a party to the event
    and it is not the case that the party has accepted the event
then the event breaches the rule every_party_accepts_first.

when the ledger applies an event
    and the event takes effect on a date
    and the current date is a second date
    and the date is later than the second date
then the event breaches the rule not_before_the_effective_date.

when the ledger applies an event
    and the event has been applied
then the event breaches the rule only_once.

a party and a second party are covered by a master agreement if
    the party and the second party have a master agreement.

a party and a second party are covered by a master agreement if
    the second party and the party have a master agreement.

